## Skill 1: Getting the model to write its own prompts

.image figures/prompt-writing.svg 180 _

- The commands are saved prompts — markdown files in .claude/commands/
- Every issue in my repositories was drafted by a model
- **Demo:** /make-work proposes the batch; I approve; /gh-issue-push files it
- **→ switch to GitHub** and read what the model wrote for its future self:

.link https://github.com/petar-djukic/sdd-hello-world/issues today's demo repo: petar-djukic/sdd-hello-world
.link https://github.com/Nokia-Bell-Labs/declarative-agents/issues and at scale: Nokia-Bell-Labs/declarative-agents, 1,500+ issues

: Passing mention, not a section: the specs live in files, not scrollback — breaking the work down for the model is its own skill, write-up on the take-home slide. Gate 1 on screen: nothing exists until the human approves the plan. Show the command file itself briefly — just markdown. The issue is a prompt the model wrote for its future self.

## Skill 1, live: plan a backlog on the hello-world repo

.code prompt-make-work.txt

- Two commands, one gate: nothing exists until the plan is approved

: make-work takes no arguments — the scope line is what keeps it to an epic and two issues instead of the whole roadmap. Full loop verified 2026-08-10: epic #85 → PR #88 merged → reset #84 reverted it.

## Skill 2: Long-term memory management

.image figures/agents.svg 240 _

- The executing session reads the issue body and the repository, and nothing else
- Nothing from the planning conversation survives, and nothing needs to
- The shape is forty years old: a blackboard — agents are the specialists, GitHub is the board, the control component is you (Hayes-Roth 1985)
- **Demo:** /do-work — implement, validate, PR opens. The agent never merges its own PR; I review and merge. Done means closed.

: Gate 2 on screen: validation is the contract. The state outlives the tool — the opencode slide after Skill 3 proves it. Fallback: narrate from a rehearsal run.

## Skill 2, live: pop the issue, do the work

.code prompt-pop-work.txt

- The pop decomposes and isolates; the work session starts cold and reads the board

: The worktree the pop creates is the next slide's subject — point at the cd line when you get there. First rehearsal produced PR #83 (closed by reset); current epic is #85.

## Skill 3: Giving every task its own workspace

.image figures/article-worktrees-layout.svg 260 _

- A worktree is born when the task starts and dies when the PR merges
- Disposable workspaces mean parallel agents, safe breakage, and your checkout stays yours
- **Demo:** remote branches and sub-issue progress on GitHub — then `git worktree list` in the terminal

.link https://github.com/petar-djukic/sdd-hello-world/branches remote branches: sdd-hello-world/branches

## Skill 3, live: opencode picks up the work

.code prompt-opencode.txt

- The tool is disposable; the loop is not

: The riskiest beat — rehearse it first. Fallback: narrate the command file and the GitHub state; everything the tool needs is there.

## Skill 4: Giving the agent temporal agency

.image figures/article-recurring-loop.svg 220 _

- A work order that outlives its completion: closing the issue creates the next instance
- The upgrade issue has tracked this repo's tooling since March — eight runs in its log
- Nobody remembers to do this chore; the issue remembers for you
- **Demo:** close the upgrade issue, watch the next instance open with today's line in Previous Runs

.link https://github.com/petar-djukic/sdd-hello-world/issues the recurring issue: sdd-hello-world/issues

## Skill 4, live: reset the demo with a recurring issue

.code prompt-reset.txt

- The reset is itself a work order — closing it schedules the next one

: Issue #89 — reset has run twice (#79, #84), both in its Previous Runs log. Every rehearsal appends a Previous Runs line — by talk day the issue demonstrates the pattern with real history.

